title: $:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-notebook/sticky
tags: [[$:/tags/Stylesheet]]
code-body: yes

<!--
Sticky tiddler titles.

This lives in its own stylesheet tiddler rather than in base.tid because it
needs widgets: a reveal to honour the option, and a list to generate the
z-index ladder. base.tid restricts wikitext parsing with `\rules only` and so
cannot contain either.

The ladder is the non-obvious part. Once titles are sticky, a title scrolling
inside its own frame will pass UNDER the frame that follows it in the DOM,
because later siblings paint on top by default. Counting z-index downwards per
position reverses that, so each title stays above the tiddlers below it. 100
steps is the practical ceiling that Vanilla settled on.

The offset is --nb-menubar-h, NOT --nb-story-top.

Those two differ in kind. --nb-story-top is a layout offset: it pushes the
story river down inside the document, and scrolling consumes it — by the time
a title is sticking, the storytop gap has already scrolled off the screen.
Adding it here would hold the title that much too low forever, leaving a band
of dead space above every stuck title.

--nb-menubar-h is the part that is pinned to the VIEWPORT. That is the only
thing a sticky element has to clear, because it is the only thing still there
after scrolling. With no menubar the value is 0 and titles stick to the top of
the page, whatever storytop happens to be.

The negative inline margins let the title's background span the sheet's full
padding, so body text scrolls under an opaque strip instead of appearing in
the gutter beside it.
-->

<%if [{$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-notebook/options/stickytitles}match[yes]] %>
``
.tc-tiddler-title {
	position: sticky;
	inset-block-start: var(--nb-menubar-h, 0px);
	z-index: 500;
	background-color: ``<<colour tiddler-background>>``;

	/*
	Widen the strip from the sheet's content box out to its padding box, so
	body text scrolls under an opaque band rather than appearing beside it.

	These must mirror .tc-tiddler-frame's padding EXACTLY. The frame's extra
	inline-start padding is unconditional — it reserves the margin gutter
	whether or not the rule is drawn in it — so this compensation has to be
	unconditional too. Scoping it to .nb-margin-rule left the strip 34px short
	on the inline start whenever the rule was switched off.

	Negative margins only widen a box whose inline-size is auto. Anything here
	that sets an explicit width silently turns this back into a shift.
	*/
	margin-inline: calc(-1 * (var(--nb-margin) + var(--nb-space-lg))) calc(-1 * var(--nb-space-lg));
	padding-inline: calc(var(--nb-margin) + var(--nb-space-lg)) var(--nb-space-lg);
	padding-block: 2px;
}

/*
A narrow river collapses the sheet to an even --nb-space-md padding and drops
the margin rule (see the `@container river` block in base.tid). The strip has
to follow that, or it overhangs the sheet by the gutter it no longer has.
*/
@container river (max-width: 34rem) {

	.tc-tiddler-title {
		margin-inline: calc(-1 * var(--nb-space-md));
		padding-inline: var(--nb-space-md);
	}
}

/*
The sheet's margin rule is drawn by .tc-tiddler-frame::before, which sits
BEHIND the title. Once the title is sticky it carries an opaque background
across the full width of the sheet — including the gutter — so it erases the
rule wherever it comes to rest, leaving a gap in the line.

The title therefore draws its own segment. Because the title is pulled back to
the sheet's padding edge by the negative margin above, an offset of
--nb-margin from its own box lands on exactly the same column as the frame's
rule, and the two read as one continuous line.

`position: sticky` is itself a positioned value, so the title is already a
containing block for this pseudo-element.
*/
.nb-margin-rule .tc-tiddler-title::before {
	content: "";
	position: absolute;
	inset-block: 0;
	inset-inline-start: var(--nb-margin);
	inline-size: 1px;
	background-color: var(--nb-margin-rule);
	pointer-events: none;
}
``
<$list filter="[range[100]]">
`.tc-story-river .tc-tiddler-frame:nth-child(100n+`<$text text=<<currentTiddler>>/>`) {
	z-index: `<$text text={{{ [[200]subtract<currentTiddler>] }}}/>`;
}
`
</$list>
<% endif %>
